🛠️ Tools and Technologies Used
    AutoCAD Map 3D: For designing and managing DWG files with precise layouts.
    ArcGIS: For spatial adjustments, annotations, and shapefile creation.
    QGIS: For converting DXF to shapefile and KML, and styling spatial data.
    Google My Maps: For publishing and sharing interactive maps online.


📋 Workflow and Steps

DWG File Import in AutoCAD Map 3D
  Designed and managed real estate layouts in DWG format for accuracy.
  (Files available in the Data/CAD_Files folder)

Spatial Adjustment in ArcGIS
  Imported DWG files and performed georeferencing and spatial adjustments to ensure precise alignment with real-world coordinates.
  (Step-by-step instructions available in Configurations/ArcGIS_Adjustments.md)

Data Conversion in QGIS
  Converted DXF files to GIS-ready shapefiles and styled them for visualization.
  Exported the final layers to KML format for integration with Google My Maps.
  (QGIS workflow guide in Configurations/QGIS_Styling.md)

Publishing Maps in Google My Maps
  Uploaded KML files to Google My Maps for interactive visualization.
    Created two maps with distinct modes:
        Owner Mode: Comprehensive map with detailed property insights.
        Public Mode: Simplified map highlighting available plots.

Interactive Visualization
    Published maps allow users to filter plots, query details, and interact with spatial data seamlessly.


💡 Key Learnings
  Converting CAD-based real estate layouts to GIS improves spatial accuracy and accessibility.
  GIS tools like QGIS and ArcGIS streamline data processing and visualization.
  Google My Maps is an effective and user-friendly platform for sharing spatial data with stakeholders.



